// Test: `hasServerField` on a resolver root fragment's split operation.
//
// The flag answers one question — can this resolver produce a value before the
// response is normalized? It cannot if its root fragment reads any server field,
// because that data does not exist yet. The exec-time executor uses it to decide
// whether to evaluate the resolver while building the execution tree, or to
// leave it to the post-normalization drain.
//
// It is a property of the ROOT FRAGMENT, not of the return type, so the resolver
// here returns a scalar and no client-edge machinery is involved.
//
// This fixture covers the positive case only. This harness emits a split
// operation for a resolver root fragment only when that fragment normalizes
// something, so a client-only root fragment produces no artifact here and the
// negative case cannot be observed. It is covered instead against real generated
// artifacts, where both kinds exist side by side — see
// `TestSchemaThreadFieldWithClientRootFragment$normalization.graphql.js`
// (client-only, no flag) next to
// `TestSchemaServerUserGreetingFragment$normalization.graphql.js` (S2C, flag).

//- User_server_backed.js
/**
 * @relayField User.server_backed: String
 * @rootFragment UserServerBackedFragment
 */
graphql`fragment UserServerBackedFragment on User {
  name
}`

//- relay.config.json
{
  "language": "typescript",
  "schema": "schema.graphql",
  "resolversSchemaModule": {
    "path": "__generated__/ResolversSchemaModule.js"
  }
}

//- schema.graphql
type Query { me: User }
type User { name: String }
